","Stimulation of arterial baroreceptors leads to an increase in the activity of neurons in the nucleus tractus solititus (NTS). This occurs because arterial baroreceptors are distressible organs that respond to changes in blood pressure. When blood pressure increases, the receptors are stretched, causing them to release vasodilator agents (e.g., norepinephrine) and increase their sympathetic neuron firing. These signals are transmitted through the sympathetic nervous system to the nucleus tractus solititus (NTS), where they are interpreted and processed. The resulting sympathetic neuron firing leads to a decrease in blood pressure and blood pressure regulation.
