Lecithins are soluble in organic solvents, such as chloroform, alcohol, and ether. They are insoluble in water due to their amphiphilic nature. Lecithins are amphipathic molecules, meaning they have both hydrophilic (water-loving) and hydrophobic (water-repelling) parts. The hydrophilic part is the polar head group, while the hydrophobic part is the nonpolar fatty acid chains. In water, the hydrophobic tails tend to cluster together, while the hydrophilic heads face the solvent. This arrangement is not energetically favorable, leading to insolubility. 

Thus, the correct answer is $\boxed{\text{water}}$.

