","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔS = ΣnS(products) - ΣnS(reactants)

where n is the number of moles of each substance and S is the standard entropy of the substance.

For the reaction:

2 CO(g) + O2(g) → 2 CO2(g)

The standard entropies (S°) at 298 K are:

S°(CO) = 197.6 J/(mol·K)
S°(O2) = 205.1 J/(mol·K)
S°(CO2) = 213.8 J/(mol·K)

Now, we can plug these values into the equation:

ΔS = [2 × S°(CO2)] - [2 × S°(CO) + S°O2]

ΔS = [2 × 213.8 J/(mol·K)] - [2 × 197.6 J/(mol·K) + 205.1 J/(mol·K)]

ΔS = [427.6 J/(mol·K)] - [790.2 J/(mol·K)]

ΔS = -362.6 J/(mol·K)

Since we are considering 3 moles of CO, we need to multiply the ΔS value by 3:

ΔS = -362.6 J/(mol·K) × 3

ΔS = -1087.8 J/K

The change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction is -1087.8 J/K.
