What is the difference between isotopologues and isotopomers?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","Isopopologues and isotopomers are two terms used to describe variations in molecules based on isotopic substitutions. The key difference lies in whether the substitution affects the molecular structure or just the mass. 

1. **Isopologues**: Isopologues are molecules that have the same molecular formula but differ in the isotopic composition of their atoms. For example, \( \text{C}_2\text{H}_2 \) and \( \text{C}_2\text{D}_2 \) are isopologues because they have the same molecular formula but differ in the presence of deuterium (D) instead of hydrogen (H). Isopologues are chemically similar but may have different physical properties, such as chemical shift in NMR spectroscopy or infrared absorption spectra.

2. **Isottomers**: Isottomers are molecules that have the same molecular formula and the same connectivity of atoms but differ in the isotopic composition of their atoms. For example, \( \text{C}_2\text{H}_2 \) and \( \text{C}_2\text{H}^{13}\text{C} \) are isotomers because they have the same connectivity (C=C) but differ in the presence of \( ^{13}\text{C} \) instead of \( ^1\text{C} \). Isottomers are chemically identical but can be distinguished by techniques like mass spectrometry.

In summary, the key difference is that isopologues can have different connectivity (e.g., \( \text{C}_2\text{H}_2 \) and \( \text{C}_2\text{D}_2 \)) while isotomers must have the same connectivity (e.g., \( \text{C}_2\text{H}_2 \) and \( \text{C}_2\text{H}^{13}\text{C} \)).
