","Absolute asymmetric synthesis is a method of producing a single enantiomer of a chiral molecule with at least than 99.9% enantiomeric excess. This means that the reaction produces almost exclusively one enantiomer of the product, with the other enantiomer being present in negligible amounts. This is distinct from other types of asymmetric synthesis, where the reaction produces a mixture of enantiomers, though in unequal amounts. The key difference lies in the extent of enantiomeric excess.

For example, in a reaction where the product is 90% one enantiomer and 10% the other, this is considered asymmetric synthesis but not absolute asymmetric synthesis. In contrast, if the product is 99.9% one enantiomer and 0.1% the other, this is considered absolute asymmetric synthesis.

To achieve absolute asymmetric synthesis, chemists often use chiral catalysts or reagents that selectively interact with one enantiomer of the substrate, leading to the formation of only one enantiomer of the product. This can be achieved through the use of chiral ligands or catalysts, which can be chiral molecules or can be designed to have chiral properties.

An example of absolute asymmetric synthesis is the synthesis of the amino acid D-phenylalanine using the Sharpless asymmetric epoxidation reaction. In this reaction, a chiral catalyst (titanium(IV) isopropoxide with a chiral tartrate ligand) is used to selectively epoxidze the substrate, leading to the formation of a single enantiomer of the product. The reaction is highly enantioselective, with an enantiomeric excess of 99.9% or higher.

In summary, while all asymmetric synthesis involves the formation of enantiomers in unequal amounts, absolute asymmetric synthesis is the most efficient and efficient form, producing one enantiomer with an extremely high degree of purity.
