   - If \( R_i(t) \) is the reliability of component \( i \).  

b) **Parallel Structure**:  
   - The system fails only if all components fail.  
   - The reliability \( R_S(t) \) is the complement of the probability that all components fail:  
     \[
     R_S(t) = 1 - (1 - R_1(t)) \cdot (1 - R_2(t)) \cdot (1 - R_3(t)) \cdots (1 - R_n(t))
     \]  
   - If \( R_i(t) \) is the reliability of component \( i \).  

c) **Combination of Serial and Parallel Structures**:  
   - The reliability is calculated by considering the serial and parallel stages separately and combining their relibilities.  
   - For example, if the system consists of two serial stages, each with \( n \) parallel components, the reliability is:  
     \[
     R_S(t) = R_{S1}(t) \cdot R_{S2}(t)
     \]  
     where \( R_{S1}(t) \) is the reliability of the first serial stage (product of parallel component relibilities) and \( R_{S2}(t) \) is the reliability of the second serial stage (product of parallel component relibilities).  

   - The general case involves breaking the system into smaller serial and parallel stages and applying the above rules sequentially.  

**Note**: The reliability functions \( R_i(t) \) are piecewise functions that describe the probability that component \( i \) is operational at time \( t \).  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{a) Serial: } R_S(t) = R_1(t) \cdot R_2(t) \cdot R_3(t) \cdots R_n(t) \\
&\text{b) Parallel: } R_S(t) = 1 - (1 - R_1(t)) \cdot (1 - R_2(t)) \cdot (1 - R_3(t)) \cdots (1 - R_n(t)) \\
